{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"triumphant-feline-drongo","creation_channel":"cli","description":"`update_priority` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py:417`) accepts a\nkeyword-only `acli_cmd: list[str] | None = None` and never reads it. The caller at\n`adapters/jira/acli.py:180` passes it **by keyword**:\n\n```python\nacli_cli_ops.update_priority(jira_key, priority_name, acli_cmd=acli_cmd)\n```\n\nThe sibling path in the same module does honour it — `_verify_created_issue(result.stdout,\nacli_cmd=acli_cmd, client=client)` at `:410`.\n\n## Be precise about what is and is not wrong\n\nThe function's docstring explains it deliberately bypasses ACLI:\n\n> ACLI does not support priority edit — uses direct REST API:\n> PUT /rest/api/3/issue/{key} with {\"fields\":{\"priority\":{\"name\":\"...\"}}}\n\nSo `acli_cmd` — an ACLI invocation vector — is **structurally inapplicable** to this function, not\nmerely unimplemented. The confirmed defect is therefore a signature that advertises a parameter it\ncannot use, with a caller passing it as though it matters. That is the same false-promise shape as\n`7504-a127-e9b0-42bb`.\n\n## The open question that decides severity\n\nInstead of using anything the caller supplied, the body resolves credentials ambiently:\n\n```python\n_s = acli_subprocess.resolve_jira_settings()\njira_url, user, api_token = _s.url, _s.user, _s.api_token\n```\n\n**If `acli_cmd` can encode a non-default Jira target** (a different site, instance, or credential\nset), then a deployment that configures one target would have priority updates go to the *ambient*\ntarget while every other write goes to the configured one — a cross-target write, which is a\ncorrectness bug rather than signature hygiene. **If `acli_cmd` only ever carries the ACLI binary\npath or invocation flags**, this reduces to removing a dead parameter.\n\nThis was NOT determined. Resolve it first; the remediation follows from the answer. Do not assume\nthe benign reading.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Determine whether `acli_cmd` can encode a Jira target distinct from what\n      `resolve_jira_settings()` returns; record the finding on this ticket with a citation\n- [ ] If it can: priority updates resolve their target the same way the rest of the adapter does,\n      and a test pins that a non-default configured target is honoured\n- [ ] If it cannot: `acli_cmd` is removed from `update_priority`'s signature and from the call site\n      at `acli.py:180`\n- [ ] Either way, no function in this module accepts a parameter it cannot act on\n- [ ] The docstring states how this REST path resolves credentials, since it differs from the\n      ACLI-based siblings around it","id":"c9c6-a713-8bc6-4088","parent_id":"b5bc-f4f7-3433-4181","priority":2,"tags":["janitor"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"update_priority accepts acli_cmd and cannot use it; credential resolution is ambient"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786055577101728001,"uuid":"6a30ce88-c967-4306-aed7-c56210a43c12"}